    "nodes": [
      {
        "node_id": "QUIN-NODE-ENTRY",
        "node_kind": "ENTRY_CHECK_BOUNDARY",
        "primary_kc_id": "V3-KC-271440",
        "secondary_kc_refs": [],
        "route_eligibility": [
          "STANDARD"
        ],
        "learner_content_blocks": [
          "Starting check: distinguish oxidation from reduction by electron bookkeeping and identify carbonyl versus phenolic hydroxyl groups."
        ],
        "representation_ids": [
          "QUIN-REP"
        ],
        "activity_ids": [
          "QUIN-ACT-ENTRY"
        ],
        "checkpoint_ref": "QUIN-CP-ENTRY",
        "source_claim_refs": [
          "QUIN-CLAIM"
        ]
      },
      {
        "node_id": "QUIN-NODE-ORIENTATION",
        "node_kind": "ORIENTATION",
        "primary_kc_id": "V3-KC-271440",
        "secondary_kc_refs": [],
        "route_eligibility": [
          "STANDARD"
        ],
        "learner_content_blocks": [
          "Goal: recognize the oxidized quinone and reduced hydroquinone forms, write their net two-electron redox relationship, and locate the redox-active part of ubiquinone.",
          "Bridge: oxidation means net electron loss and reduction means net electron gain. Structural cues make that bookkeeping visible."
        ],
        "representation_ids": [
          "QUIN-REP"
        ],
        "activity_ids": [],
        "checkpoint_ref": "QUIN-CP-ORIENTATION",
        "source_claim_refs": [
          "QUIN-CLAIM"
        ]
      },
      {
        "node_id": "QUIN-NODE-EXPLANATION",
        "node_kind": "EXPLANATION",
        "primary_kc_id": "V3-KC-271440",
        "secondary_kc_refs": [],
        "route_eligibility": [
          "STANDARD"
        ],
        "learner_content_blocks": [
          "A para-quinone head group contains two carbonyl groups on the ring. Net two-electron, two-proton reduction converts those carbonyl oxygens to two hydroxyl groups, giving the hydroquinone form.",
          "The bounded net relationship is Q + 2 e− + 2 H+ ⇌ QH2. Moving Q → QH2 is reduction; moving QH2 → Q is oxidation. The equation tracks overall stoichiometry even when a biological system transfers the equivalents in controlled steps.",
          "Ubiquinone has a redox-active quinone head group and a hydrophobic isoprenoid tail. The tail supports membrane localization; it is not the site that changes between the oxidized and fully reduced head-group forms."
        ],
        "representation_ids": [
          "QUIN-REP"
        ],
        "activity_ids": [],
        "checkpoint_ref": "QUIN-CP-EXPLANATION",
        "source_claim_refs": [
          "QUIN-CLAIM"
        ]
      },
      {
        "node_id": "QUIN-NODE-MODEL",
        "node_kind": "MENTAL_MODEL",
        "primary_kc_id": "V3-KC-271440",
        "secondary_kc_refs": [],
        "route_eligibility": [
          "STANDARD"
        ],
        "learner_content_blocks": [
          "Use a head-and-tail model: inspect the ring oxygens for redox state, count net electron/proton uptake or release at the head, and treat the hydrocarbon tail as a localization feature."
        ],
        "representation_ids": [
          "QUIN-REP"
        ],
        "activity_ids": [],
        "checkpoint_ref": "QUIN-CP-MODEL",
        "source_claim_refs": [
          "QUIN-CLAIM"
        ]
      },
      {
        "node_id": "QUIN-NODE-THINK",
        "node_kind": "EXPERT_THINK_ALOUD",
        "primary_kc_id": "V3-KC-271440",
        "secondary_kc_refs": [],
        "route_eligibility": [
          "STANDARD"
        ],
        "learner_content_blocks": [
          "If the ring shows two C=O groups, I identify Q, the oxidized form. To reach QH2, I place 2 e− and 2 H+ on the reactant side and convert both carbonyl oxygens to hydroxyls. I do not assign the redox change to the isoprenoid tail because its bonding pattern is unchanged in the pair."
        ],
        "representation_ids": [
          "QUIN-REP"
        ],
        "activity_ids": [],
        "checkpoint_ref": "QUIN-CP-THINK",
        "source_claim_refs": [
          "QUIN-CLAIM"
        ]
      },
      {
        "node_id": "QUIN-NODE-WORKED",
        "node_kind": "WORKED_EXAMPLE",
        "primary_kc_id": "V3-KC-271440",
        "secondary_kc_refs": [],
        "route_eligibility": [
          "STANDARD"
        ],
        "learner_content_blocks": [
          "Worked example: a membrane carrier changes from a ring with two carbonyls to the corresponding ring with two hydroxyls while the tail remains unchanged. The carrier has accepted two electrons and two protons overall, so the head group was reduced from ubiquinone to ubiquinol."
        ],
        "representation_ids": [
          "QUIN-REP"
        ],
        "activity_ids": [],
        "checkpoint_ref": "QUIN-CP-WORKED",
        "source_claim_refs": [
          "QUIN-CLAIM"
        ]
      },
      {
        "node_id": "QUIN-NODE-GUIDED",
        "node_kind": "GUIDED_PRACTICE",
        "primary_kc_id": "V3-KC-271440",
        "secondary_kc_refs": [],
        "route_eligibility": [
          "STANDARD"
        ],
        "learner_content_blocks": [
          "Guided practice: mark the two ring oxygens, classify each as carbonyl or hydroxyl, then place 2 e− and 2 H+ on the correct side of the net equation."
        ],
        "representation_ids": [
          "QUIN-REP"
        ],
        "activity_ids": [
          "QUIN-ACT-GUIDED"
        ],
        "checkpoint_ref": "QUIN-CP-GUIDED",
        "source_claim_refs": [
          "QUIN-CLAIM"
        ]
      },
      {
        "node_id": "QUIN-NODE-LIGHT",
        "node_kind": "LIGHT_SUPPORT_PRACTICE",
